1
00:00:04,500 --> 00:00:09,467
An engineer automates a bisection overnight.
A bisection is Git's binary search over

2
00:00:09,507 --> 00:00:14,355
commits, the saved snapshots of a project.
In the morning the tool reports a first

3
00:00:14,395 --> 00:00:17,574
bad commit, with full confidence
and a full commit ID.

4
00:00:17,614 --> 00:00:21,806
The commit adds a command-line option.
It doesn't touch scoring at all.

5
00:00:22,146 --> 00:00:27,299
The search wasn't broken. The test was.
The script returned the runner's own exit

6
00:00:27,339 --> 00:00:32,428
status, the number a program hands back
when it ends: 0 when the score passed, 1

7
00:00:32,468 --> 00:00:37,685
when it didn't. A crash also exits with 1.
So the first commit that crashed for an

8
00:00:37,725 --> 00:00:42,686
unrelated reason was recorded as bad,
Git searched among its ancestors, and it

9
00:00:42,726 --> 00:00:48,008
reported the commit that introduced the
crash. The textbook's words for that answer

10
00:00:48,048 --> 00:00:52,624
are: precise, reproducible and wrong.
Keep that innocent commit in mind.

11
00:00:52,664 --> 00:00:53,522
It comes back.

12
00:00:55,362 --> 00:00:59,062
Welcome back to Git and GitHub
Deep Mastery. Pull up a chair.

13
00:00:59,102 --> 00:01:03,906
In the previous video you bisected by
hand and gave five verdicts and one skip.

14
00:01:03,946 --> 00:01:08,505
Answering by hand is error-prone and
doesn't scale past a handful of steps.

15
00:01:08,545 --> 00:01:13,226
Today the verdicts come from a program,
and the contract between Git and that

16
00:01:13,266 --> 00:01:18,193
program is three numbers. You'll also
search for a fix instead of a bug, save and

17
00:01:18,233 --> 00:01:21,688
replay a session, and go through
the pitfalls one by one.

18
00:01:23,528 --> 00:01:27,490
write a test script whose exit
status follows the bisect protocol;

19
00:01:27,830 --> 00:01:30,189
automate a bisection with git bisect run;

20
00:01:30,529 --> 00:01:33,709
use custom terms for a search
that is not about good and bad;

21
00:01:34,049 --> 00:01:36,005
save and replay a bisect log;

22
00:01:36,345 --> 00:01:41,379
explain what it means when bisect names
a merge commit, and when --first-parent

23
00:01:41,419 --> 00:01:41,764
helps.

24
00:01:43,604 --> 00:01:48,738
🟡 CAUTION: git bisect run <cmd> runs a
command at every step and takes the verdict

25
00:01:48,778 --> 00:01:53,595
from its exit status. It carries the
same label as the manual subcommands: it

26
00:01:53,635 --> 00:01:58,769
detaches HEAD, which leaves you on no
branch, checks out other commits, and writes

27
00:01:58,809 --> 00:01:59,841
the bisect state.

28
00:02:00,181 --> 00:02:05,501
The protocol has four rows. Exit
status 0 means good, or old.

29
00:02:05,541 --> 00:02:10,071
Exit status 1 to 127, except
125, means bad, or new.

30
00:02:10,111 --> 00:02:15,255
Exit status 125 means this
commit can't be tested: skip it.

31
00:02:15,295 --> 00:02:20,439
Anything else, such as 128 and
above, aborts the bisection.

32
00:02:20,779 --> 00:02:25,067
Try it now, on paper. Thirty seconds:
the runner crashes at one commit.

33
00:02:25,107 --> 00:02:29,882
Which exit status should your script
return, and which did the overnight script

34
00:02:29,922 --> 00:02:31,345
return? Say your answer.

35
00:02:36,185 --> 00:02:41,603
It should return 125. The overnight
script returned 1, and 1 means bad.

36
00:02:41,943 --> 00:02:46,054
Why three outcomes and not two? Because
the question has three answers.

37
00:02:46,094 --> 00:02:50,556
A test for bisect must distinguish "has
the property", "does not have it" and

38
00:02:50,596 --> 00:02:55,408
"cannot tell". That's the lesson of the
opening, and it's the reason the number 125

39
00:02:55,448 --> 00:02:55,817
exists.

40
00:02:56,157 --> 00:03:00,784
Where the script lives matters. The manual
advises keeping the test outside the

41
00:03:00,824 --> 00:03:05,747
repository, "to prevent interactions between
the bisect, make and test processes and

42
00:03:05,787 --> 00:03:10,591
the scripts". A tracked script, one stored
in the repository, would be replaced by

43
00:03:10,631 --> 00:03:14,313
each commit's own version of it,
or be absent in older commits.

44
00:03:14,653 --> 00:03:19,256
Terms. "Good" and "bad" are awkward
when you're looking for an improvement.

45
00:03:19,296 --> 00:03:24,395
old and new are built-in alternatives, and
--term-old and --term-new let you choose

46
00:03:24,435 --> 00:03:28,357
your own words. The subcommands
are then named after your words.

47
00:03:28,397 --> 00:03:33,496
For git bisect run the mapping stays
positional: exit status 0 means the old state,

48
00:03:33,536 --> 00:03:34,796
whatever it's called.

49
00:03:35,136 --> 00:03:38,754
Log and replay. The output of
git bisect log is replayable.

50
00:03:38,794 --> 00:03:43,404
Save it, and a session can be repeated,
handed to a colleague, or repaired.

51
00:03:43,444 --> 00:03:48,302
If you gave a wrong verdict: save the
log, delete the wrong line and everything

52
00:03:48,342 --> 00:03:51,960
after it, run git bisect reset,
and replay the edited file.

53
00:03:52,000 --> 00:03:56,176
You continue from the last correct
verdict instead of starting over.

54
00:03:56,516 --> 00:04:01,393
More controls. Paths after two dashes
restrict the search to commits that touch

55
00:04:01,433 --> 00:04:06,497
those paths: fewer steps when you know the
area. --first-parent follows only first

56
00:04:06,537 --> 00:04:11,477
parents, so a regression that arrived
through a merge is attributed to the merge

57
00:04:11,517 --> 00:04:16,021
commit. --no-checkout doesn't touch
the working tree and sets a ref named

58
00:04:16,061 --> 00:04:19,071
BISECT_HEAD for tests that
read objects directly.

59
00:04:19,411 --> 00:04:24,478
That form is 🟢 SAFE. git bisect skip accepts
a revision or a range, to mark commits

60
00:04:24,518 --> 00:04:29,093
untestable in advance. git bisect
visualize shows the remaining candidates.

61
00:04:29,133 --> 00:04:33,770
And git bisect reset <commit> ends the
session on that commit instead of the

62
00:04:33,810 --> 00:04:34,755
starting branch.

63
00:04:35,095 --> 00:04:40,474
When to use --first-parent: when the commits
inside merged branches aren't required

64
00:04:40,514 --> 00:04:45,894
to work on their own. On screen, a feature
branch, B and C, merged into main by the

65
00:04:45,934 --> 00:04:51,379
merge commit M. With --first-parent the
search never visits B and C, so a fault they

66
00:04:51,419 --> 00:04:53,011
carry is attributed to M.

67
00:04:53,351 --> 00:04:58,315
When not to bisect at all, from section
14A.26. Don't bisect what you can read.

68
00:04:58,355 --> 00:05:03,382
Don't bisect before you have checked that
the known-good commit is still good in

69
00:05:03,422 --> 00:05:08,259
today's environment. And one warning
that belongs to security: git bisect run

70
00:05:08,299 --> 00:05:13,009
executes whatever the old commits
contain, build scripts and test harnesses

71
00:05:13,049 --> 00:05:18,329
included. On an untrusted repository that
is arbitrary code execution, so do it in a

72
00:05:18,369 --> 00:05:18,836
sandbox.

73
00:05:20,676 --> 00:05:24,113
Keep the dictionary from the
last video, and add a clerk.

74
00:05:24,153 --> 00:05:29,053
You no longer look at the page yourself.
A clerk looks and holds up a card: green

75
00:05:29,093 --> 00:05:33,871
for "before", red for "after", and a third
card that says "this page is torn, I

76
00:05:33,911 --> 00:05:34,847
cannot read it".

77
00:05:35,187 --> 00:05:38,146
A clerk with only two cards
has to lie about torn pages.

78
00:05:38,186 --> 00:05:39,645
That's the two-outcome test.

79
00:05:39,985 --> 00:05:44,570
Where the model breaks: the clerk is your
program running inside old checkouts of

80
00:05:44,610 --> 00:05:49,366
your own project. It's affected by what is
installed, cached and compiled around the

81
00:05:49,406 --> 00:05:53,820
tracked files. The textbook's sentence
is: a commit is only its tracked files.

82
00:05:53,860 --> 00:05:56,732
The environment around them
is your responsibility.

83
00:05:58,572 --> 00:06:01,748
Row one and row two are the
two halves of the search.

84
00:06:01,788 --> 00:06:06,664
Row three is the torn page. Row four is
the safety net: a status that no ordinary

85
00:06:06,704 --> 00:06:10,184
test returns stops the run
instead of producing a verdict.

86
00:06:10,524 --> 00:06:15,496
When bisect names M and both C and E are
good, neither side introduced the fault.

87
00:06:15,536 --> 00:06:17,475
The fault is in the combination.

88
00:06:19,315 --> 00:06:25,295
Exit 0 if the score line shows exact_match=0.800.
Exit 1 if there is a score line

89
00:06:25,335 --> 00:06:31,017
with another value. Exit 125 if the
runner can't produce a score line at all.

90
00:06:31,057 --> 00:06:34,881
Notice the path: one directory
above the repository.

91
00:06:34,921 --> 00:06:40,231
The whole search is now two commands.
Predict which commit it will name.

92
00:06:40,271 --> 00:06:42,238
Say it out loud. I'll wait.

93
00:06:50,078 --> 00:06:56,560
d926d3c again. The same sequence of commits
as your manual session, without a human

94
00:06:56,600 --> 00:06:59,390
verdict. Now check what the 125 did.

95
00:06:59,730 --> 00:07:05,346
The line for 074d492 says skip. The crash
was recorded as "no verdict", not as bad.

96
00:07:05,386 --> 00:07:09,094
That's the overnight mistake
from the opening, avoided.

97
00:07:09,434 --> 00:07:12,127
The same test fits on one
line, with a weakness.

98
00:07:12,467 --> 00:07:17,394
The exit status of grep is the verdict. It
works here because the range was chosen

99
00:07:17,434 --> 00:07:20,908
to contain no crashing commit.
A crash would count as bad.

100
00:07:20,948 --> 00:07:23,452
Use the one-liner only when you know that.

101
00:07:23,792 --> 00:07:29,730
Now two failures. Quick quiz, three options.
Is an exit status of 200 recorded as

102
00:07:29,770 --> 00:07:34,379
bad, recorded as a skip, or does
the run stop? Say it out loud.

103
00:07:39,219 --> 00:07:44,587
It stops. "bisect run failed": the code is
outside the protocol and the run stops.

104
00:07:44,627 --> 00:07:49,995
And a mistyped path. Shells return 127 for
"command not found", which the protocol

105
00:07:50,035 --> 00:07:51,182
would read as bad.

106
00:07:51,522 --> 00:07:56,684
Git 2.55 doesn't take 126 or 127
at face value on the first step.

107
00:07:56,724 --> 00:08:03,086
It runs the command on the known good
commit as well, and stops when it gets the

108
00:08:03,126 --> 00:08:07,807
same code there: "bogus exit
code 127 for 'good' revision".

109
00:08:08,147 --> 00:08:11,476
One more failure from the same
script: a dirty working tree.

110
00:08:11,816 --> 00:08:18,445
An uncommitted edit in a file that differs
between the commits to visit, and the

111
00:08:18,485 --> 00:08:22,197
checkout is refused.
Start from a clean tree.

112
00:08:28,337 --> 00:08:33,805
The subcommands are broken and fixed now.
For the run, think positionally: exit 0

113
00:08:33,845 --> 00:08:39,312
must mean the old state, which here is
"broken". grep -q KeyError exits 0 when it

114
00:08:39,352 --> 00:08:40,400
finds the crash.

115
00:08:40,740 --> 00:08:42,866
"be9ad1b4894348317c1e7ccb8276b8ad4760fb89
is the first 'fixed' commit".

116
00:08:42,906 --> 00:08:43,935
Save the session before you end it.

117
00:08:58,875 --> 00:09:02,104
The replay reaches the same
commit without running one test.

118
00:09:02,144 --> 00:09:06,517
This file is what you hand to a colleague,
and what you edit when one verdict was

119
00:09:06,557 --> 00:09:06,844
wrong.

120
00:09:07,184 --> 00:09:09,702
Within one session the
vocabulary is fixed.

121
00:09:10,042 --> 00:09:13,674
Now --first-parent. Predict
the candidate count.

122
00:09:14,014 --> 00:09:18,718
Eighteen candidates instead of twenty,
and a different first commit to test,

123
00:09:18,758 --> 00:09:23,586
be9ad1b. --first-parent changes the
candidate set, and with it the path of the

124
00:09:23,626 --> 00:09:24,023
search.

125
00:09:24,363 --> 00:09:26,380
And the start that is the wrong way round.

126
00:09:26,720 --> 00:09:31,566
Git checks that every good commit is an
ancestor of the bad one, and says so when

127
00:09:31,606 --> 00:09:32,109
it's not.

128
00:09:38,949 --> 00:09:40,597
Five mistakes to watch for.

129
00:09:40,937 --> 00:09:46,995
A two-outcome test. Root cause: a crash and
a wrong result both exit non-zero, so an

130
00:09:47,035 --> 00:09:51,714
untestable commit is recorded as
bad; exit 125 for "cannot tell".

131
00:09:52,054 --> 00:09:57,023
A test script tracked in the repository.
Root cause: each checkout replaces it with

132
00:09:57,063 --> 00:10:00,886
that commit's version, or removes
it in commits that predate it.

133
00:10:01,226 --> 00:10:05,932
A flaky or environment-dependent test. Root
cause: bisect accepts every verdict as

134
00:10:05,972 --> 00:10:10,794
fact and converges on an innocent commit
without complaint; fix seeds, pin the data,

135
00:10:10,834 --> 00:10:15,367
compare against a threshold with a margin,
and rebuild what is derived from the

136
00:10:15,407 --> 00:10:16,640
sources at every step.

137
00:10:16,980 --> 00:10:21,841
Custom terms with the exit statuses
reversed. Root cause: for git bisect run the

138
00:10:21,881 --> 00:10:25,884
mapping is positional; 0 is the
old state whatever word you chose.

139
00:10:26,224 --> 00:10:31,415
Bisecting in a shallow clone. Root cause:
bisect can only visit commits that exist

140
00:10:31,455 --> 00:10:36,391
locally, and in a clone made with --depth,
as CI checkouts often are, the good

141
00:10:36,431 --> 00:10:38,497
commit may not be present at all.

142
00:10:40,337 --> 00:10:45,314
Now, out of the lab. A team evaluating an
LLM application sees answer quality on a

143
00:10:45,354 --> 00:10:50,392
regression set drop after a week of merges.
The evaluation takes twenty minutes and

144
00:10:50,432 --> 00:10:52,962
its score varies slightly from run to run.

145
00:10:53,302 --> 00:10:58,354
They apply the three rules of section
14A.21. They make the test as small as the

146
00:10:58,394 --> 00:11:03,509
symptom allows: twelve prompts that separated
last week's build from today's, not

147
00:11:03,549 --> 00:11:08,282
the full suite. They make it deterministic:
fixed seeds, pinned data, and a

148
00:11:08,322 --> 00:11:12,292
threshold with a margin between
the good score and the bad one.

149
00:11:12,332 --> 00:11:17,638
And they rebuild what is derived from the
sources at every step: installed packages,

150
00:11:17,678 --> 00:11:22,411
compiled extensions, caches. The script
exits 125 when the package can't be

151
00:11:22,451 --> 00:11:23,048
installed.

152
00:11:23,388 --> 00:11:29,631
Bisect names a merge commit. Both parents
pass. On the picture, that's M, with its

153
00:11:29,671 --> 00:11:35,837
parents E and C. So nothing on either side
is wrong by itself: one side renamed a

154
00:11:35,877 --> 00:11:40,970
prompt template field and the other
added a caller of the old name.

155
00:11:41,010 --> 00:11:45,031
The merge was textually clean
and semantically wrong.

156
00:11:45,071 --> 00:11:50,241
They look at the combined result of
the merge, not at either branch.

157
00:11:52,081 --> 00:11:56,966
Your turn. Do Lab 11.5, "An
automated git bisect run", in

158
00:11:57,006 --> 00:12:00,076
lab-manual/m11-history-forensics.md.

159
00:12:00,416 --> 00:12:05,101
The lab runs the two-outcome test first.
Before you run it, predict which commit it

160
00:12:05,141 --> 00:12:09,599
will name and why that commit is wrong.
Then write the three-outcome script and

161
00:12:09,639 --> 00:12:12,389
predict which line of git
bisect log will change.

162
00:12:12,729 --> 00:12:19,717
The challenge is Exercise 11.11, Level 5,
"Retrieval quality dropped on Friday", in

163
00:12:19,757 --> 00:12:23,443
exercises/m11-m15-investigation-recovery.md.

164
00:12:25,283 --> 00:12:26,550
Pause and answer out loud.

165
00:12:31,390 --> 00:12:37,723
A strong answer doesn't treat
the result as a tool failure.

166
00:12:37,763 --> 00:12:46,688
It says what the result means about where
the fault is, using the vocabulary of the

167
00:12:46,728 --> 00:12:55,654
merge videos: textually clean, semantically
wrong. It says what to examine next and

168
00:12:55,694 --> 00:13:01,703
how, in terms of the merge's
result against each parent.

169
00:13:02,043 --> 00:13:06,819
It separates this case from the one where
--first-parent was used on purpose, in

170
00:13:06,859 --> 00:13:10,852
which a merge is the expected answer
and stands for a whole branch.

171
00:13:10,892 --> 00:13:15,728
And it includes the check that applies to
every surprising bisect result: confirm

172
00:13:15,768 --> 00:13:19,521
the verdicts at the reported
commit and at its parents by hand.

173
00:13:21,361 --> 00:13:24,288
Let's land this. You
should now be able to say:

174
00:13:24,628 --> 00:13:32,307
git bisect run reads the exit status: 0 is
the old state, 1 to 127 except 125 is the

175
00:13:32,347 --> 00:13:36,442
new state, 125 is skip,
anything else aborts.

176
00:13:36,782 --> 00:13:41,005
The test script lives outside the tree
being tested and must be deterministic.

177
00:13:41,345 --> 00:13:44,573
Custom terms rename the subcommands; they
do not change which exit status means old.

178
00:13:44,913 --> 00:13:49,641
git bisect log is a replayable record:
I can save it, edit it and replay it.

179
00:13:49,981 --> 00:13:56,308
A merge named as the culprit, with both
parents good, means the fault is in the

180
00:13:56,348 --> 00:13:57,275
combination.

181
00:13:59,115 --> 00:14:06,479
Read sections 14A.21, 14A.22 and
14A.25 to 14A.27 of Chapter 14A.

182
00:14:06,519 --> 00:14:10,693
Then do the Practice section, 14A.29.

183
00:14:11,033 --> 00:14:15,880
Today you handed the verdicts to a script,
and you know the three numbers that keep

184
00:14:15,920 --> 00:14:20,296
it honest. Write the three-outcome
script in the lab before the next video.

185
00:14:20,336 --> 00:14:25,124
Next time: what Git keeps, and the reflog.
Until then, look at the state first and

186
00:14:25,164 --> 00:14:27,303
type second. See you in the next one.
